{
  "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
  "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Bryan S. Gowdy)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Sentencing an adolescent to life without any possibility of parole condemns him to die in prison and rejects any hope that he will change for the better. This sentence, like the death penalty, cruelly ignores the inherent qualities of youth and the differences between adolescents and adults."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Bryan S. Gowdy)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We are -- we are drawing the line, Your Honor, at -- at non-homicide cases because we recognize under the Eighth Amendment that we must look at societal consensus, and society has said that murder is different and has said that in the sentencing practices, as demonstrated by the fact that outside of"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Are you urging that in all cases, including homicide cases? Or are you drawing the line at homicide?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When a sentence is imposed rarely, it becomes unconstitutional?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Bryan S. Gowdy)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our position is that you are looking at two things. One, is it cruel? It's cruel because life without parole is unique, is particularly cruel to adolescents because it -- it gives up on the adolescent and determines that he is forever unfit to live in civil society."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Various correctional officers",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would point you to the amicus brief filed by the various correctional officers that talk about the types of programs that can be done."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What studies do you have to -- that -- that comment on that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Bryan S. Gowdy)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0000-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A juvenile is -- not only does he have an inherent capacity to grow; he is less culpable. And so to exact the most -- for a non-homicide crime whether you are adult or juvenile, this is the most severe punishment you can receive, and to exact that most severe punishment for a less culpable offender that the Court has recognized is a less culpable offender doesn't -- is too much retribution. We are not saying the State can't exercise retribution, but that life without parole is -- is too much for those types of crimes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: The logic in Roper was very straightforward. It says, \"Death is reserved for the worst of the worst.\" I think that was the quote."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Is there -- is there disproportionality review generally in Florida and particularly for juvenile offenders?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gowdy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOWDY: There is no -- no. Under Florida law, there is no basis to challenge a sentence as being excessive or disproportionate as long as it's at the statutory maximum."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Do you think that it categorically violates the Eighth Amendment for a 10-year-old to be sentenced to life without parole?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But isn't it true -- and I think one of my colleagues already questioned you -- that the prosecutor didn't think that this merited life without parole."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Did he -- how do you answer the argument that unlike an adult, because of the immaturity, you can't really judge a person -- judge a teenager at the point of sentencing? That it's only after a period of time has gone by, and you see, has this person overcome those youthful disabilities? That's why a proportionality review on the spot doesn't accommodate the -- what is the driving force of the -- your -- the Petitioner's argument is that you can't make a judgment until years later to see how that person has -- has done."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-7412",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fab9c3ab0fe78aa5e9831855dbf027ed605fa3176d3fdd7e1c4c35b65aabe9a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fab9c3ab0fe7-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can you tell us just a little bit about the Florida correctional systems, the policies with respect to rehabilitation programs? If they don't have parole, then you might say, well, they don't need rehabilitation programs or that they might need them more."
    },
    {
